{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If a soul sins.</b> We find three expressions concerning those who sin: כִּי, אִם, אֲשֶׁר. Concerning an individual who sins it is written ״נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״, and concerning many who sin it is stated (Leviticus 4:13) ״וְאִם כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁגּוּ״, and concerning the prince it is written (Leviticus 4:22) ״אֲשֶׁר נָשִׂיא יֶחֱטָא״. And the commentators said that the expression ״כִּי״ indicates greater certainty than the expression אִם, and the expression אֲשֶׁר indicates greater certainty than the expression כִּי. Therefore, concerning the sin of the many — which is a thing that is not common, that many would agree upon a sin — for that reason it is stated ״אִם כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל יִשְׁגּוּ״, which indicates doubt. And so concerning the anointed priest it is stated (Leviticus 4:3) ״אִם הַכֹּהֵן הַמָּשִׁיחַ יֶחֱטָא״, for it is remote and uncommon that the anointed priest or the many would sin. But an ordinary individual easily comes to sin; therefore it is stated ״נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״ — for the expression ״כִּי״ indicates a thing close to certain that it will be so. But the prince who exalts himself above every head and conducts his princedom with pride — from the height of his heart he will surely come to sin; therefore it is stated ״אֲשֶׁר נָשִׂיא יֶחֱטָא״, for the expression אֲשֶׁר indicates a thing that will definitely happen and contains no doubt. And so you will find in the Zohar and in Rabbeinu Bachye and in Toldot Yitzchak. And it is difficult for me on this matter: if so, why is it stated (Leviticus 4:27) ״וְאִם נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת תֶּחֱטָא״ — why did he mention concerning the individual sometimes the expression אִם and sometimes the expression כִּי? And further it is difficult: why did he say ״נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת״ — for when he says ״נֶפֶשׁ״ we know that it is one and not two? And if it is the way of Scripture to say ״נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת״, if so why did he say ״נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״ and not say אַחַת?" "And it appears to say that according to the way of the world, every individual who sins unwittingly sins in matters where he sees that many stumble and are not careful concerning that thing; therefore he errs to say that no offence will befall him in that matter. And it is the rarest of the rare that there is some individual who sins even in a matter where he does not see many stumbling in that matter, and he is alone in that matter — and this is something uncommon and his mind is external. Therefore it is stated (Leviticus 4:27) ״וְאִם נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת תֶּחֱטָא״ — that is, the one sinning should be a single soul alone and have no likeness or companion among the creatures; this is something uncommon, therefore the expression אִם is stated concerning it, which indicates doubt, in the manner stated concerning the anointed priest and the Sanhedrin. But when he said ״נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״ he did not mention the expression אַחַת, because this speaks of an individual who sins in matters where he sees that many are not careful concerning them; therefore the thing is common and close to certain that he too will stumble in it, therefore the expression כִּי is stated concerning it. And a proof for this is what is stated ״וְאִם נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת תֶּחֱטָא בִשְׁגָגָה מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ״ — that is, that he is distinct in this sin from the people of the land: he does not see others doing and then do; but concerning the expression כִּי it is not stated מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ." "And by way of allusion one may say that therefore it is stated ״בִשְׁגָגָה מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ״ — to tell you that specifically concerning an am ha'aretz it is reckoned as unwitting error, but the unwitting error of a Torah scholar is accounted as intentional, and this is a correct allusion. And that which is stated ״וְאִם נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תֶחֱטָא״ (Leviticus 5:17) — he mentioned both אִם and כִּי, because this speaks of an asham talui, and he mentioned the expression כִּי in it because it speaks of an individual for whom it is close to certain that he will come to sin. However, from the side of the sin itself he mentioned the expression אִם, for such a sin is not common — that there should be before him two pieces, one of fat and so forth. Another thing: therefore it is stated ״וְאִם נֶפֶשׁ אַחַת תֶּחֱטָא״, because this speaks of an individual who separates himself from the community, as it is stated ״מֵעַם הָאָרֶץ״ — that he separates himself from the people of the land and does not sit among the people of the land, for he fears lest he learn from their deeds, and in this he is not close to certain that he will come to sin; therefore the expression אִם is stated, which indicates doubt."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:25.345301280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}